1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =='''Dorthea (Dorothea) Mørch (c. 1769 - after 1832)'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biographical Notes:'''
2 CONT In 1807, '''Dorthea''' moved to Forstad (Fredrikstad) and became a merchant, operating a tavern and a dance hall. She was known as a high-spirited and temperamental woman who was not afraid to use "either words or fists." Her busi
2 CONC ness often faced legal issues regarding licensing and closing hours. Despite the scandals, her brother '''Lars Mørch''' supported her by lending her money for court fines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical records by W. Lassen indicate she was still living in 1832.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriages and Family:'''
2 CONT '''Dorthea''' was married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Henrik Sønderdahl''' (1755–1799). Married on Feb 17, 1785. Children: '''Knud''' (1785), '''Johanne Marie''' (1789), and [https://www.geni.com/people/Eleonore-Louise-Movig-Sønderdal/6000000037133752889?through=6000000002278466
2 CONC 504 '''Eleonore Louise'''] (1798–1853).
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Ole Eriksen Berg'''. Married on Aug 18, 1802. Children: '''Erik Henrik''' (1802) and likely '''Karen Marie''' (1808).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources:'''
2 CONT # [https://slekt1.com/familien-morch-fra-melsomvik-og-smaalenene-1923/ Mørch, Gustav. Familien Mørch: Fra Melsomvik og Smaalenene. Kristiania, 1923.]
2 CONT # [https://www.nb.no/items/da3ddac70eee13f1a55f4e48213546a1 Dehli, Martin. Fredrikstad bys historie. 2: Fra festningsby til handelssenter: 1767–1860. Fredrikstad, 1964.] P. 67, 130-131.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1807, as a widow after her first husband, Dorothea Sønderdal moved to Forstad (a district in Fredrikstad) where she began trading. She started with street vending and also ran a bar and a dance hall [2, p. 67].
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothea's life was marked by certain difficulties and scandals. She was described as a temperamental woman who was not afraid to use either words or her fists [2, p. 67]. During a trial, it emerged that her inn was often full of 
2 CONC people even after ten o'clock in the evening, despite warnings from the law officers [2, p. 67]. The parish priest also testified that Dorothea's reputation "was not entirely spotless," and that she lacked a license to run an inn 
2 CONC or act as a street vendor [2, p. 67].
2 CONT
2 CONT After one of the scandals in court, Dorothea expressed remorse for her behavior, which "completely conflicts with the way of thinking and acting of her esteemed family (Mørch)" [2, p. 130]. Her brother, Lars Mørch, who held great 
2 CONC authority in society, likely strongly disliked that the family was compromised by his sister's behavior [2, p. 131]. Despite this, he lent her money to pay the sums ordered by the court [2, p. 131].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''See reference number 2 in the source list above.'''
