2 CONT Nicolai Wergeland (father)
2 CONT Henrik Wergeland (brother)
2 CONT Camilla Collett (sister)
2 CONT Harald Nicolai Storm Wergeland (cousin, Lieutenant General)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Other work'''
2 CONT cartographer, teacher, skiing pioneer, landscaper
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Personal life'''
2 CONT Wergeland was born in Christianssand[1] and grew up at Eidsvold. He was the son of priest and politician Nicolai Wergeland, and a brother of the poet Henrik Wergeland and feminist writer Camilla Collett.[1] His cousin was military
2 CONC  officer Harald Nicolai Storm Wergeland. In 1859 he returned to Christianssand, where he stayed the rest of his life.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Career'''
2 CONT Joseph Frantz Oscar Wergeland photographed in the 1860s
2 CONT Wergeland became a military officer in 1834, a First Lieutenant in 1839, Lieutenant Colonel in 1866, Colonel in 1868, and Major General in 1880.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wergeland had many talents. He worked 11 years as a cartographer for the Norwegian Mapping and Cadastre Authority, where he drew the first official Norwegian county maps (the first issued was Christians Amt in 1845). He drew the f
2 CONC irst large and detailed map of Norway for use in schools.[3] He was a teacher in drawing and calligraphy at the Norwegian Military Academy. He was orderly officer for the King. He was a member of the committee for the Hoved Line, 
2 CONC Norway's first railway line, and several road constructions. As an eager sportsman and skier, he initiated military skiing divisions, and wrote a book about the history of skiing and its military applications.[4] He is regarded as
2 CONC  the founder of the sports clubs Christianssand ski- og skøyteklubb (1862) and Oddersjaa (1875).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT A significant number of letters from the Wergeland family is preserved, largely due to Oscar, who kept the letters he received from other family members.[1] He had a good relationship with his sister Camilla, and her articles from
2 CONC  travels to European cities inspired his landscape work in Christianssand, as he constructed the Wergeland park and the Ravnedalen park,[3] planted Baneheia (former grazing land) with trees by use of soldiers, and transformed seve
2 CONC ral main roads into large avenues.[1] The Wergeland Park was constructed in 1859–60.[5] It is surrounded by many old and important buildings, such as the Cathedral (1696/1737/1885), Latinskolen (1736/1855) and the City Hall (1864)
2 CONC .[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Cliffs, Ravnedalen Park'''
2 CONT The Ravnedalen Park was constructed in the 1870s as a romantic garden, in a valley with dramatic cliffs, waterfalls and lake.[3] It has open areas for public arrangements like concerts, gatherings and festivals.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Statue'''
2 CONT Gustav Lærum's bronze statue of Wergeland in Ravnedalen Park (above) was unveiled in 1917.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Books'''
2 CONT * Skiløbingen, dens historie og krigsanvendelse (1865)[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT * May-Brith Ohman Nielsen. "Oscar Wergeland som familiemedlem" (in Norwegian). Wergeland 2008. Archived from the original on 11 July 2011. Retrieved 11 November 2008.
2 CONT * "General Oscar Wergelands hus" (in Norwegian). UIA. Archived from the original on 24 July 2011. Retrieved 11 November 2008.
2 CONT * Jahn Holljen Thon, based on May-Brith Ohman Nielsen. "Oscar Wergeland (1815-1895) og Ravnedalen" (in Norwegian). Wergeland 2008. Retrieved 15 November 2008.
2 CONT * O. Wergeland, Oberstløitnant (1865). Skiløbningen, Dens historie og krigsanvendelse (in Norwegian). Retrieved 11 November 2008.
2 CONT * Jahn Holljen Thon, based on May-Brith Ohman Nielsen. "Wergelandsparken" (in Norwegian). Wergeland 2008. Archived from the original on 24 July 2011. Retrieved 15 November 2008.
2 CONT * Wikborg, Tone. "Gustav Lærum". In Helle, Knut (ed.). Norsk biografisk leksikon (in Norwegian). Oslo: Kunnskapsforlaget. Retrieved 27 May 2011.
