2 CONT
2 CONT LARCHMONT, N. Y., March 9.- Hermann Fougner, a widely known Norwegian engineer, who built concrete ships for the government during the World War, died this morning at the New York Medical Centre of ailments resulting from a fall i
2 CONC n January. He was 55 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Fougner, who resided at 23 Chestnut Avenue, Larchmont Manor, was born in Norway. He was graduated from the Technical School at Trondkhen, Norway, in 1895, having come to this country soon thereafter. One of his first positions
2 CONC  here was that of assistant chief engineer of the American Steel and Foundry Company. He was later on the engineering staff of Milliken Brothers. In 1900 Mr. Fougner represented the Russian Government in China and was in charge of
2 CONC  that government's work at Port Arthur, where he constructed a dry dock.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1905 until 1918 he was the New York representative of the Truscon Steel Company and during the World War went into the construction of concrete ships for the United States Government and the Standard Oil Company. He founded t
2 CONC he Fougner Steel Company, of which he was president and chief designer. This company went out of existence in 1922.
2 CONT
2 CONT A widow, Mrs. Stella Gray Fougner, whom he married in San Diego, Cal., in 1903, and two brothers, Nicolai, a New York engineer, and Waldemar, an engineer of Oslo, Norway, and two sisters, both of Oslo, survive. A funeral service w
2 CONC ill be held on Saturday afternoon at 2:15 o'clock at St. John's Episcopal Church, Larchmont. Burial will be in Kensico Cemetery. [THE NEW YORK TIMES, March 10, 1932]
